{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 5, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by external threats and internal discord. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab and this experience shaped his diplomatic approach toward Muslim neighbors while he governed his own territories with considerable skill. He acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and Raymond managed state affairs with prudence during a time when the kingdom faced constant pressure from Saladin and other rising powers.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond entered the world as the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem yet his path to power was fraught with danger since his father was assassinated by the Order of Assassins in 1152. Young Raymond fell into the hands of Nur ad-Din Zangi shortly after taking his title and he remained imprisoned in Aleppo for roughly ten years before paying a massive ransom to secure his freedom. This long period of confinement allowed him to learn Arabic customs and develop a nuanced understanding of Islamic politics that would later define his foreign policy decisions throughout his reign as count.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe leper king Baldwin IV appointed Raymond as regent because the monarch could not rule alone due to his deteriorating health condition. Raymond sought peace with Saladin and he arranged truces that bought valuable time for the fragile Christian states even though some factions within the court viewed his actions as overly conciliatory or even treasonous. Tensions escalated between Raymond and Guy of Lusignan since the latter married Sibylla and pursued aggressive policies that threatened the stability Raymond worked hard to maintain through careful negotiation and strategic alliances with local Muslim rulers.\n\n*Final Years and Legacy*\nRaymond eventually reconciled with Guy of Lusignan shortly before the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Christian army suffered a crushing defeat against Saladin forces. Raymond survived the battle by escaping the field while many others perished or were captured and he retreated to Tripoli where he died later that same year possibly from pleurisy or despair over the collapse of the kingdom he tried to save. His death marked the end of an era of pragmatic diplomacy in the Crusader states and left a vacuum that no subsequent leader could fill effectively during the ensuing crises. Historians often view him as one of the most capable leaders of his generation because he understood the limitations of Crusader power and sought realistic solutions rather than engaging in reckless holy wars that would invite destruction upon his people.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -8.702316e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0003582881}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.055865508}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.093622155}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -6.4851956e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.1457695e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.0994463e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -8.583106e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.017303115}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.00904556